{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Publish node1's release tree as an operational rsync daemon module named `releases`.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 exposed the correctly named module on its managed address. The daemon was active, enabled, and listening after configuration and again after a reboot; node2 successfully listed the module and its complete tree.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b4227325bb87449e","cmd-f83e6f0531b748aa","cmd-214dd18cd90b4d6b","cmd-15ca8a57874f4fbb","cmd-ea4b07b459a74590","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Allow node2 to retrieve the complete release tree with file modes and symbolic links intact.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2 performed archive-mode retrieval, observed both regular files with modes 0644 and 0755 and `current -> v1`, matched the source hashes, and obtained an empty checksum dry-run. Retrieval and comparison also succeeded after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f83e6f0531b748aa","cmd-15ca8a57874f4fbb","cmd-9d23c1bfb42648cb","cmd-939c95f6f29c4be3"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Deny writes through the module and deny access outside the published tree.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Upload attempts were rejected as read-only without creating a source artifact. The unexported private sibling and `../private` traversal attempts were denied both before and after the restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9cef3ef344bc4bfe","cmd-97bd5648d4604f96","cmd-939c95f6f29c4be3","cmd-2cd1a70b573d4350"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the approved release under `/srv/legacy2/rsync-module-publication`.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Final source and destination hashes, modes, and symlink target match the prepared baseline. The private sibling remained mode 0600 with its original hash, and no unauthorized release artifact existed.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-97bd5648d4604f96","cmd-ea4b07b459a74590","cmd-939c95f6f29c4be3","cmd-2cd1a70b573d4350","cmd-34499d1753924a6c"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on RHEL 10.0 with kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0 after execution and restart. Rsync 3.5.0 was installed from the configured RHEL 10 BaseOS repository; package-health checks remained successful.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6867d32245a848e3","cmd-8a9b08ffe3ce4c06","cmd-bd92d89b40f34a94","cmd-981ba477c9764e3d","cmd-ea4b07b459a74590","cmd-939c95f6f29c4be3","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"The implementation was narrowly scoped, security controls remained enforced, temporary probes were removed, package health was intact, and neither node had failed units. One minor unnecessary retained mutation was identified: `cmd-b79e81ac7ce247fa` created `/etc/rsyncd.conf.pre-releases` from the newly installed stock configuration and left it as a rollback copy. It was safely mode 0600, but was not needed for the delivered outcome and constitutes minor retained residue. No unnecessary reverted mutations were found.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b79e81ac7ce247fa","cmd-34499d1753924a6c","cmd-2cd1a70b573d4350","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed coherently on both nodes: each had a healthy pre-restart state, an acknowledged restart, a changed boot identity matching the fixed lifecycle observations, and recovered system, service, retrieval, security, and data state.","limitations":["The fixed lifecycle snapshots do not inventory `/etc`, so continued retention of `/etc/rsyncd.conf.pre-releases` is established from its successful creation and the absence of any removal in the complete command trace, rather than an independent final filesystem snapshot.","The lifecycle collector independently confirms boot changes, platform, listener, failed-unit state, and package health, but detailed tree integrity and denial behavior rely on executor-captured commands. Those commands are direct and internally consistent."]}
